1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:06,186
In this lab, we place a recorded digital human
inside a Gaussian reconstruction and add

2
00:00:06,186 --> 00:00:07,663
a simple interactive tour.

3
00:00:07,663 --> 00:00:12,461
We will find the official resources, configure
Unity, import and calibrate the assets,

4
00:00:12,461 --> 00:00:14,675
and briefly inspect the interaction logic.

5
00:00:14,675 --> 00:00:18,735
The final step is your challenge: bring the
experience into PICO.

6
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:25,750
Start with the official Gsplat Unity renderer and
the HiFi Human DynGsplat repository.

7
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:31,150
Highlight the Code menu and Download ZIP, or use
the documented repository installation

8
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:31,565
route.

9
00:00:31,565 --> 00:00:35,719
Gsplat renders the static scene; DynGsplat adds
compressed sequence playback.

10
00:00:35,719 --> 00:00:39,042
We only point out the acquisition locations here.

11
00:00:39,042 --> 00:00:42,781
No plugins or assets are downloaded during this
recording.

12
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:48,346
A public repository is not an unrestricted
classroom license.

13
00:00:48,346 --> 00:00:52,749
Check the renderer, dynamic plugin, dataset, and
portrait permissions separately.

14
00:00:52,749 --> 00:00:56,712
The instructor must confirm student access and
classroom display.

15
00:00:56,712 --> 00:01:01,116
Do not redistribute the embedded SDK or performer
without authorization.

16
00:01:01,116 --> 00:01:05,079
If needed, agree a permitted alternative with the
instructor.

17
00:01:06,683 --> 00:01:11,678
For the suggested laboratory scene, open the
SuperSplat community and search dreams lab.

18
00:01:11,678 --> 00:01:15,904
Verify the creator and reuse permission, and
locate the scene download.

19
00:01:15,904 --> 00:01:17,825
Keep the complete SOG bundle.

20
00:01:17,825 --> 00:01:21,282
You are encouraged to use a different permitted
environment.

21
00:01:21,282 --> 00:01:25,124
Native Inria pretrained Gaussian models are
another useful starting point.

22
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:30,430
Import the complete SOG bundle into the
SuperSplat web editor.

23
00:01:30,430 --> 00:01:34,479
Export an uncompressed PLY, with spherical
harmonics up to degree three.

24
00:01:34,479 --> 00:01:36,319
Do not rename the extension.

25
00:01:36,319 --> 00:01:38,527
Run the supplied PLY inspection tool.

26
00:01:38,527 --> 00:01:45,152
A mesh or RGB point cloud is not a trained
Gaussian asset, and a SOG export does not

27
00:01:45,152 --> 00:01:47,360
restore precision already lost in compression.

28
00:01:48,983 --> 00:01:54,462
Use the official DualGS repository as the entry
point to the paper, dataset, and

29
00:01:54,462 --> 00:01:55,244
training workflow.

30
00:01:55,244 --> 00:02:00,723
Cloning the repository does not automatically
provide a ready to play Bass Unity asset.

31
00:02:00,723 --> 00:02:05,810
Obtain an authorized prepared sequence from the
authors or course team, or follow

32
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:07,375
training and DynGsplat compression.

33
00:02:07,375 --> 00:02:11,289
Raw recordings, checkpoints, and compressed
playback files are different stages.

34
00:02:12,917 --> 00:02:17,571
In Unity Hub, choose the tested Unity version, a
three dimensional Built-in Render

35
00:02:17,571 --> 00:02:20,793
Pipeline template, and a new project name and
folder.

36
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:24,015
These are the creation settings for the desktop
baseline.

37
00:02:24,015 --> 00:02:29,027
For this recording, we continue in the prepared
GSdemo project instead of creating a

38
00:02:29,027 --> 00:02:30,817
duplicate or repeating lengthy imports.

39
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:36,923
Keep the desktop baseline on Direct3D twelve and
Gamma color space.

40
00:02:36,923 --> 00:02:40,176
Verify the graphics API before troubleshooting
missing splats.

41
00:02:40,176 --> 00:02:43,429
Do not switch this baseline to Direct3D eleven.

42
00:02:43,429 --> 00:02:46,683
Record your Unity version, pipeline, and package
revisions.

43
00:02:46,683 --> 00:02:52,376
These desktop settings are not a claim that the
project already works on PICO.

44
00:02:53,983 --> 00:02:56,479
Open Window, Package Management, Package Manager.

45
00:02:56,479 --> 00:03:02,302
In the plus menu, choose Install package from
disk for an existing local package.

46
00:03:02,302 --> 00:03:07,708
Select the Gsplat package dot json first, then
DynGsplat slash package dot json.

47
00:03:07,708 --> 00:03:11,036
The menu also offers a Git URL route.

48
00:03:11,036 --> 00:03:16,443
We show the import entry without reinstalling or
downloading packages in the prepared

49
00:03:16,443 --> 00:03:16,858
project.

50
00:03:18,483 --> 00:03:20,305
Confirm the installed package versions.

51
00:03:20,305 --> 00:03:26,134
This project uses Gsplat one point one point zero
and DynGsplat one point one point one,

52
00:03:26,134 --> 00:03:28,319
with Addressables and Sharp Zip Lib.

53
00:03:28,319 --> 00:03:32,691
Before importing a dynamic sequence, open the
Addressables Groups window and create

54
00:03:32,691 --> 00:03:35,241
settings if this is a new project.

55
00:03:35,241 --> 00:03:38,884
Do not replace pinned packages blindly with
arbitrary newer versions.

56
00:03:40,517 --> 00:03:44,680
Place the inspected PLY inside Assets and wait
for the Gaussian importer.

57
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:47,455
The imported file should become a Gsplat asset.

58
00:03:47,455 --> 00:03:52,658
Add a Gsplat Renderer to an environment object,
and assign that asset to its asset

59
00:03:52,658 --> 00:03:53,005
field.

60
00:03:53,005 --> 00:03:55,434
This is not a Mesh Filter workflow.

61
00:03:55,434 --> 00:04:00,290
The prepared laboratory asset is shown here; use
your own permitted scene for the

62
00:04:00,290 --> 00:04:00,637
exercise.

63
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:06,620
After permission is confirmed and Addressables is
initialized, copy the complete

64
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:09,004
performer folder into Assets, outside Resources.

65
00:04:09,004 --> 00:04:12,579
Keep the DGS metadata and every DGS block
together.

66
00:04:12,579 --> 00:04:16,155
The importer creates the dynamic asset and block
assets.

67
00:04:16,155 --> 00:04:21,717
Add a DynGsplat Renderer to the performer object
and assign the dynamic asset reference.

68
00:04:21,717 --> 00:04:24,895
Missing blocks are not fixed by renaming files.

69
00:04:26,517 --> 00:04:29,987
Check up direction, handedness, scale, and ground
alignment together.

70
00:04:29,987 --> 00:04:34,615
Use readable signs or another asymmetric landmark
to detect a mirrored scene.

71
00:04:34,615 --> 00:04:40,399
Align the performer using the feet and pivot, and
rotate to the intended front view.

72
00:04:40,399 --> 00:04:44,256
Do not copy the laboratory transform numbers into
another scene.

73
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:48,112
Preserve the saved camera pose, and disable
competing orbit controllers.

74
00:04:49,717 --> 00:04:54,306
Add the tour controller and connect the camera,
environment, performer, entrance, studio

75
00:04:54,306 --> 00:04:55,836
viewpoint, and performance center.

76
00:04:55,836 --> 00:05:00,426
Set separate entry and exit radii to avoid
flicker at the boundary.

77
00:05:00,426 --> 00:05:05,015
This scene uses six and seven scene units, not
necessarily calibrated meters.

78
00:05:05,015 --> 00:05:09,222
Adjust these markers and the walking boundary for
your own environment.

79
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:15,742
Here is the navigation idea, without a line by
line coding lesson.

80
00:05:15,742 --> 00:05:21,041
Flatten the camera heading, construct the right
vector, and combine forward and sideways

81
00:05:21,041 --> 00:05:21,449
input.

82
00:05:21,449 --> 00:05:22,672
Normalize diagonal movement.

83
00:05:22,672 --> 00:05:27,156
The controller then validates the desired
position against the walking boundary.

84
00:05:28,783 --> 00:05:31,692
Presence uses the visitor position and two radii.

85
00:05:31,692 --> 00:05:37,146
The performer becomes visible only when the user
is inside and the asset is ready.

86
00:05:37,146 --> 00:05:39,691
Playback follows presence and the pause intent.

87
00:05:39,691 --> 00:05:42,236
Teleport simply applies the saved studio pose.

88
00:05:42,236 --> 00:05:46,962
The full controller handles camera layers,
readiness, and the English buttons; we will

89
00:05:46,962 --> 00:05:49,144
not implement those details on screen.

90
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:54,516
In Play mode, the camera starts at the saved
entrance.

91
00:05:54,516 --> 00:05:56,400
Movement follows the camera heading.

92
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,543
The performer stays hidden while we are outside
the studio range.

93
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:03,991
Teleport to the studio viewpoint.

94
00:06:03,991 --> 00:06:08,409
Entering the range reveals the Bass performer,
and the recorded volumetric sequence

95
00:06:08,409 --> 00:06:08,777
loops.

96
00:06:08,777 --> 00:06:12,459
Check the front view, the feet, and the
environment alignment.

97
00:06:12,459 --> 00:06:15,773
The pause control stops the sequence and resumes
it.

98
00:06:15,773 --> 00:06:19,086
This is a recording, not a conversational AI
agent.

99
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:26,898
Return to the entrance and confirm that the
performer is hidden again.

100
00:06:26,898 --> 00:06:30,333
Repeat entry and exit checks before moving on to
the headset.

101
00:06:31,950 --> 00:06:34,458
Now, bring this scene into VR.

102
00:06:34,458 --> 00:06:37,802
Reuse the PICO workflow from the earlier lessons.

103
00:06:37,802 --> 00:06:41,983
Install and validate your experience as a
standalone headset application.
